---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../../styles/chart-compare.css';
import ChartCompare from '../../../islands/ChartCompare';
import candidate from '../../../data/platform-engine-candidate.json';

const title = 'Compare Two Chart Calculations | Zodiacs.org';
const description = 'Load two calculation records and see what differs between them, and how much of it can be explained. Runs in your browser; nothing is uploaded.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/developers/compare/">
  <section class="section container container--narrow">
    <em class="kicker">Developer tools</em>
    <h1 class="display">Why do these two charts differ?</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Two programs can calculate the same birth chart and disagree. Usually the reason is
        written down in the records themselves — a different house system, a different moment,
        a different convention — and reading it off is faster than arguing about it. This page
        does that reading, and it says so when the answer is not in the files.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ChartCompare client:load />

    <h2 id="how-it-decides">How it decides</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Differences come first, because they are facts: both files are read, and every value
        that disagrees is listed. Longitudes are compared around the circle, so 359° and 1° are
        two degrees apart rather than 358, and a difference smaller than the six decimal places
        these records display is called rounding rather than a different calculation.
      </p>
      <p>Explanations are then labelled by the evidence behind them:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Reproduced</strong> — recalculated on this device, changing one setting and
          leaving everything else alone, and the result matched.</li>
        <li><strong>Reported</strong> — stated by the files themselves, with nothing to compute.</li>
        <li><strong>Hypothesis</strong> — fits, and is not demonstrated. Several can fit at once,
          and when they do you are told so instead of handed a winner.</li>
        <li><strong>Unresolved</strong> — nothing in either file accounts for it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A recalculation only ever runs on engine <code>{candidate.version}</code>, the version
        this page carries. If a record was produced by a different version it is not re-run and
        presented as the original: the difference stays a hypothesis and the limit is stated.
        A version, checksum or source URL inside an imported file is a claim that file makes
        about itself, not proof that it is genuine.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both sides of a comparison usually come from the same engine. Agreement between them
        shows the two runs are consistent; it is not an independent check of astronomical
        accuracy, and the page says so rather than letting a match imply more than it does.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="formats">What it reads, and what it does not</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Supported: the calculation record this site offers for download from a birth chart, and
        the records the <a href="/developers/examples/">developer starter</a> produces. Both are
        <code>zodiacs.natal-envelope.draft-v1</code>, validated with the engine's own parser and
        its size, depth and node limits. A record from a different engine version of that same
        schema is read, and the version difference is reported.
      </p>
      <p>
        Not supported, and not silently reinterpreted as something else: charts from other
        software in their own formats, PDFs, images and screenshots. There is no claim of
        general third-party compatibility here.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing leaves the device. The files are read in the browser, compared in memory and
        dropped when you clear them or close the page. No account, no upload, no birth details
        in the address bar, and nothing written to storage. The optional summary download leaves
        out dates, coordinates and positions, which makes it safer to share — not anonymous.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
